window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2023-09-19"]={"AD-022-23":"Resolution No. AD-022-23\nFrom: Administration Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nREQUEST TO REPURCHASE TAX LIENS AND ACCEPTANCE OF PAYMENT\nSBL 130.15-5-SAND SBL 130.15-5-15, KNOWN AS PENNSYLVANIA AVENUE AND\nDELAWARE AVENUE, NIAGARA FALLS, NEW YORK IN THE TOWN OF NIAGARA\nWHEREAS, pursuant to County Law Section 215 and Real Property Tax Law Section 902, the Niagara County Legislature approved Resolution ED-024-22 on October I 8, 2022 conveying junkyard tax liens to Niagara Orleans Land Improvement Corporation, and\nWHEREAS, the County and NORLIC executed an agreement for the conveyance of the tax liens, and\nWHEREAS, SBL 130.15-5-8 and SBL 130.15-5-15, known as Pennsylvania Avenue and Delaware Avenue, Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara were two of the properties included in the transferred tax liens, and\nWHEREAS, there is a request to redeem the properties and pay the amount of taxes owed in full, and\nWHEREAS, the County has the option pursuant to the agreement to repurchase the tax liens prior to foreclosure by NORLIC, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County is exercising its option to repurchase the tax liens from NORLIC for SBL 130.15-5-8 and SBL 130.15-5-15, known as Pennsylvania Avenue and Delaware Avenue, Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara, for one dollar, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that upon the repurchase of the tax liens, the Niagara County Treasurer is directed to accept the delinquent tax payment for SBL 130.15-5-8 and SBL 130.15-5-15, known as Pennsylvania Avenue and Delaware Avenue, Niagara Falls, New York in the Town of Niagara\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CS-033-23":"Resolution No. CS-033-23\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY YOUTH BUREAU - NEW YORK STATE AID APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has been designated as eligible to receive State Aid for 2023-24 youth programs through its Youth Bureau from the New York State Office of Children and Family Services in the amount of$255,751, $54,734, $102,280, and\nWHEREAS, the Comprehensive Planning Law mandates the Niagara County Youth Bureau must apply for such funds, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review the documents for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of the County Youth Bureau be, and hereby is, authorized to make application for the following program funds:\nYouth Development Program  $255,751\nYouth Sports and Education Opportunity  $ 54,734\nYouth Team Sports  $102,280\nTOTAL  $412,765\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute any and all documents relating to the application, subject to the review by the County Manager and the approval of the County Attorney.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CS-034-23":"Resolution No. CS-034-23\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT CENTER FOR DISEASE CONTROL FUNDS -\nEMERGENCY PLANNING - DEPARTMENT OF HEAL TH\nWHEREAS, the Emergency Planning Program of the Niagara County Department of Health provides essential services, information and training to protect the health and safety of the citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Department of Health has passed through grant funding from the Center for Disease Control (CFDA #93.323/93.354) to the Niagara County Department of Health to manage the Niagara County Portion of New York State Public Health Corps Fellowship Program, and\nWHEREAS, there is no increase cost to the county, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Health requests authorization to accept part of the $117,958 funds in 2023, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.20.4189.406 44489.11  other Health Fellowship Program  $ 67,958\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCM.20.4189.406 72100.05  Mach & Equip  $ 23,346\nCM.20.4189.406 74300.01  Travel Conference  30,958\nCM.20.4189.406 74250.01  Office Supplies  1,654\nCM.20.4189.406 74600.03  Education & Training  8,000\nCM.20.4189.406 74675.03  Print Shop Supplies  1,000\nCM.20.4189.406 74750.02  Supplies & Materials  3,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CS-035-23":"Resolution No. CS-035-23\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nBUDGET MODIFICATION -ACCEPT CENTER FOR DISEASE CONTROL\nINFRASTRUCTURE FUNDS-DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Department of Health provides essential services, information and training to protect the health and safety of the citizens of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Department of Health has passed through grant funding from the Center for Disease Control (CFDA #93.967) to the Niagara County Department of Health to Strengthen Public Health Workforce and Foundational Capabilities through 2027, and\nWHEREAS, there is no increase cost to the county, and\nWHEREAS the Department of Health requests authorization to accept part of the $774,908 funds in 2023, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCM.20.4189.426 44401.00  Public Health Federal Aid  $ 15,497\nINCREASE APPROPRJATIONS:\nCM.20.4189.426 71050.00  Overtime  $ 3,009\nCM.20.4189.426 72100.12  Mach & Equip  7,000\nCM.20.4189.426 74600.03  Education & Training  1,661\nCM.20.4189.426 74750.02  Supplies & Materials  3,200\nCM.20.4189.426 78100.00  Retirement  321\nCM.20.4189.426 78200.00  FICA  230\nCM.20.4189.426 78300.00  Worker's Comp  76\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CS-036-23":"Resolution No. CS-036-23\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nMH- BUDGET MODIFICATION - CONTRACT AGENCY STATE AID\nCOLA/MINIMUM WAGE INCREASES\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Mental Health and New York State Office of Alcoholism & Substance Abuse Services has approved additional State Aid COLA/Minimum Wage for the calendar year of2023 for various agencies, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of Community Services is hereby authorized to engage in the process to carry out the necessary agreements between the County and the various sub-contractors as aforesaid, subject to the approval of the County Attorney's Office, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE REVENUES:\nA.21.4322.412.43490.09  Mental Health Association  $ 2,131\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.01  Mental Health Programs  18,767\n(Community Missions, Dale Association, Mental Health Association,\nNew Directions, United Cerebral Palsy, Spectrum)\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.05  Reinvestment Programming  25,378\n(Community Missions, Mental Health Association,\nNew Directions, Niagara Falls Memorial Medical Center)\nCM.21.4322.415.43490.08  Community Support Services (Community Missions, MHA)  11,489\nCM.21.4322.416.43489.04  Case Management Programs  28,463\n(Community Missions, Pinnacle, New Directions,\nNiagara Falls Memorial Medical Center)\nCM.21.4322.423.43490. l 4  Supported Housing (Community Missions, DePaul)  29,727\nINCREASE APPROPRJA TIO NS:\nA.21.4322.412.74500.01  Mental Health Association  $ 2,131\nCM.21.4322.415.74500.01  Community Support Services Programs  30,256\n(Community Missions, Dale Association, Mental Health Association,\nNew Directions, United Cerebral Palsy, Spectrum)\nCM.21.4322.415.74550.06  Reinvestment Programming  25,378\n(Community Missions, Dale Association, Mental Health Association,\nNew Directions, Niagara Falls Memorial Medical Center)\nCM.21.4322.416.74500.01  Case Management Programs  28,463\n(Community Missions, Pinnacle, New Directions,\nNiagara Falls Memorial Medical Center)\nCM.21.4322.423.74500.01  Supported Housing (Community Missions, DePaul)  29,727\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-082-23":"Resolution No. CSS-082-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nPROBATION 2023 BUDGET MODIFICATION -\nACCEPT SRT BWC PROGRAM FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department has been awarded grant monies totaling $3,397.01 from the Small Rural Tribal Body Worn Camera Program, with a county match of$3,397.01,and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department wishes to continue purchasing related law enforcement equipment for the department's officers, the current grant allowing for all officers within the department to be issued a Body Worn Camera, and\nWHEREAS, the matching funds are available in the Probation Department's 2023 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be made:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\n18.3140.00044310.01  Federal Aid Probation Services General  $3,397.01\nINCREASE EXPENSE:\nA.18.3140.000.74750.05  Supplies, General Law Enforcement  $3,397.01\nSupplies\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-083-23":"Resolution No. CSS-083-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nBUILDING IMPROVEMENTS PAYMENT AND REIMBURSEMENT\nCONFLICT AND ASSIGNED COUNSEL OFFICE\nWHEREAS, the Conflict and Assigned Counsel Office previously operated on the first floor of the white house located at 169 Hawley Street, Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, the Conflict Office required additional office space as a result of the New York State Indigent Legal Services Hurrell-Harring Settlement which grant funded additional staff and attorneys in the office and as a result the department moved to the Civil Defense Building, 13 9 Hawley Street, Lockport where renovations were needed to this larger office space, and\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works performed and completed the office renovations and the Conflict Office agreed to reimburse Niagara County for the costs incurred for such renovation, not to exceed eighteen thousand dollars ($ I 8,000.00) pursuant to the grant funded contract, and\nWHEREAS, the Conflict Office has received a detailed invoice for the completion of renovations and supplies in the amount of seventeen thousand nine hundred and ninety-five dollars and seventy cents ($17,995.70), and\nWHEREAS, the Conflict Office shall receive reimbursement of the stated amount of seventeen thousand nine hundred and ninety-five dollars and seventy cents ($17,995.70) after submitting a claim to New York State Indigent Legal Services with confirmation of payment to Niagara County, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the payment and reimbursement for the building renovations: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE:\nA.04.1170.43025.03  Indigent Legal Service Hurrell-Harring Settlement $17,995.70\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED EXPENSES:\nA.04.1170.74800.03  Supplies/Services, Maintenance/Building Maint  $17,995.70\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-084-23":"Resolution No. CSS-084-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT EXTENSION OF CRITICAL INFRASTRUCTURE GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-058-21 on May 11, 2021 to accept a Critical Infrastructure grant in the amount of $50,000 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services to update and enhance the video security system at the Niagara County 911 Dispatch Center/Public Safety Training Facility for the performance period April 1, 2021 through August 31, 2022, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-049-22 on August 2, 2022 to extend the performance period of the grant to August 31, 2023, and\nWHEREAS, the work on the project has been delayed but is now scheduled, and\nWHEREAS, a budget amendment has been made to extend the Critical Infrastructure Grant program performance period until December 31, 2023, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY19 Critical Infrastructure Grant Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-085-23":"Resolution No. CSS-085-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT EXTENSION OF FY20 STONEGARDEN GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-006-21 on January 19, 2021 to accept FY20 Stonegarden grant in the amount of $275,400 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services for the performance period September 1, 2020 through August 31, 2023, and\nWHEREAS, the grant allows for expenses to assist the County in conducting border centric, intelligence driven operations with the goal of reduction or elimination of threat, risk and vulnerability along our Nation's borders, and\nWHEREAS, a grant amendment has been made to extend the FY20 Stonegarden Grant performance period until August 31, 2024, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY20 Stonegarden Grant Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-086-23":"Resolution No. CSS-086-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT EXTENSION OF FY20 EXPLOSIVE DETECTION CANINE TEAM GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-098-22 on November 15, 2022 to accept FY20 Explosive Detection Canine Team grant in the amount of $14,999 from the New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Service for the performance period October I, 2022 through August 31, 2023,and\nWHEREAS, the grant allows the Niagara County Sheriffs Office to purchase equipment and provide training to enhance explosive detection canine team capabilities, and\nWHEREAS, a grant amendment has been made to extend the FY20 Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant performance period until August 31, 2024, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the FY20 Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-087-23":"Resolution No. CSS-087-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT AID TO CRIME LABORATORIES GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded an Aid to Crime Laboratories Grant from the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services in the amount of $191,053 for the performance period July 1, 2023 through June 30, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used for expenses of crime laboratories for accreditation, training, capacity enhancement and lab related services to maintain the quality and reliability of forensic services which the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Lab provides to Niagara County agencies, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is an additional year of funding for an existing grant and is included in the 2023 budget and will be built into the 2024 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Aid to Crime Laboratories Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-088-23":"Resolution No. CSS-088-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nACCEPT HIGHWAY SAFETY GRANT FOR FORENSIC LABO RA TORY\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a Highway Safety Grant for the Forensic Laboratory from the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee in the amount of $40,000 for the performance period October 1, 2023 through September 30, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory is going to assist in raising people's awareness in drugged driving and reduce traffic fatalities by providing timely Toxicology services, participate in data sharing with ITS MR and by providing better testimony in court cases, and\nWHEREAS, the Forensic Lab will use the funds to accomplish these goals by purchasing more costly supplies for the Toxicology unit's analysis in driving involved cases as well as by sending 2 Forensic Scientists to the Borkenstein Drug Course, and\nWHEREAS, the 2023 budget needs to be amended to include the costs and revenue for the Borkenstein Drug course to be attended in 2023, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Highway Safety Grant for the Forensic Laboratory may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000 44389.07  Other Public Safety Forensic Lab  $5,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 74600.03  Training and Education  $5,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-089-23":"Resolution No. CSS-089-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE BUDGET MODIFICATION\nACCEPT FUNDING FOR NIAGARA COUNTY LAW ENFORCEMENT ACADEMY\nWHEREAS, Niagara County and Niagara County Community College are working on a joint project to construct and equip a new Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy on the Niagara County Community College Campus, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy benefits all law enforcement agencies within Niagara County for training of new recruits and continued training of existing officers, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy (NCLEA) is run jointly by the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Niagara Falls Police Department, and\nWHEREAS, the City of Niagara Falls has committed $250,000 in funding to be used to equip the new NCLEA,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, Niagara County accept the funding from the City of Niagara Falls to be used for the Niagara County Law Enforcement Academy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nH762. l 7.2497.000 42706.00  Grants from Local Government Revenue  $250,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH762.l 7.2497.000 72100.26  Technology Systems  $250,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-090-23":"Resolution No. CSS-090-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nAW ARD CONTRACT FOR RFP 2023-58 FLOOR REPLACEMENT\nAT THE JAIL POD RECREATION AREA\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature approved Resolution CW-024-23 on June 20, 2023 to fund a Capital Project to replace floors in the Niagara County Jail POD recreation yards, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Division issued Bid# 2023-58 Floor Replacement at the Jail POD Recreation Area, and\nWHEREAS, the following bid responses were received: Sierra Contracts Corp. $124,800.00; Millenium Construction $116,209.90; and Impressive Facility Services $106,000.00, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that RFP 2023-58 Floor Replacement at the Jail POD Recreation Area be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Impressive Facility Services dba Pro Squared Facility Solutions, 1243 Military Rd #2 Kenmore, NY 14217 in the amount of $106,000.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Floor Replacement at the Jail POD Recreation Area contract with Impressive Facility Services dba Pro Squared Facility Services may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-091-23":"Resolution No. CSS-091-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXTENSION OF FORENSIC FIREARMS CONSULT ANT AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-089-21 on September 21, 2021 to enter into an agreement with Cody McKellar as a consultant for forensic firearms testing and examinations for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-075-22 on September 13, 2022 to extend the Firearms Consultant Agreement with Cody McKellar for a year, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory has a continued need for an independent contractor to perform required firearms verifications and technical reviews, and\nWHEREAS, the Sheriffs Office wishes to extend the agreement with Cody McKellar for a second one-year extension period with the contract being paid out of existing funds in the 2023 Budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature has determined that the services to be provided by the consultant requires a high degree of specialized skill constituting \"professional services\" and hereby waives any requirement that additional proposals be obtained or solicited, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the County Attorney will draft an amendment for a one year extension, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Forensic Firearms Consultant Agreement extension with Cody McKellar may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-092-23":"Resolution No. CSS-092-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - STOP DWI FOUNDATION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the New York State STOP-DWI Foundation, Inc. has notified the Niagara County Sheriffs Office of an additional $5,000 in grant funds available to use for High Visibility Engagement Campaigns this year, and\nWHEREAS, the grant funds are utilized for enforcement efforts by multiple law enforcement agencies during peak holidays with the goal of reducing the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on Niagara County roads, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to modify the 2023 budget to utilize these funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3315.000 44389.09  Other Public Safety Traffic  $5,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3315.000 74400.09  Miscellaneous Expenses Payments Other Agencies  $5,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-093-23":"Resolution No. CSS-093-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT HIGHWAY TRAFFIC SAFETY GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded a Highway Traffic Safety Grant from the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee in the amount of $93,191 for the performance period October 1, 2023 through September 30, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is a continuation of an existing program and is fully funded by the New York State Governor's Traffic Safety Committee, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used to ensure that child safety seats are installed and used correctly, to educate pre-K and school aged children on bicycle and car safety, as well as to educate in order to reduce the number of crashes, injuries and deaths on the roads in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are included in the 2023 budget and will be included in the 2024 budget, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that the Traffic Safety Educator position continue to be co-terminus with the grant, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Highway Traffic Safety Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-094-23":"Resolution No. CSS-094-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nAW ARD CONTRACT FOR RFP 2023-51 FORENSIC FIREARMS/TOOL MARK EXAMINER\nTRAINER FOR NIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE FORENSIC LABO RA TORY\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Division issued RFP # 2023-51 Forensic Firearms/Tool Mark Examiner Trainer for Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory, and\nWHEREAS, the project will provide training for a Forensic Scientist I Firearms/Tool Mark Examiner to be trained for certification to perform firearms analysis for the NCSO Forensic Laboratory, and\nWHEREAS, only one bid response was received from Nichols Forensic Science Consulting Inc. in the amount of $48,950, and\nWHEREAS, the Nichols Forensic Science Consulting RFP response has been reviewed and has been found to be comprehensive and meets the needs of the NCSO Forensic Laboratory, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that RFP 2023-51 Forensic Firearms/Tool Mark Examiner Trainer for Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory be awarded to Nichols Forensic Science Consulting, Inc., 1315 B Street Antioch, CA 94509 in the amount of $48,950.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Forensic Firearms/Tool Mark Examiner Trainer for Niagara County Sheriff's Office Forensic Laboratory contract with Nichols Forensic Science Consulting, Inc. may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CSS-095-23":"Resolution No. CSS-095-23\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nOVERHEAD DOOR OF GREATER ERIE CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-091-22 on October 18, 2022 which authorized the execution of an agreement with Overhead Door of Erie to replace the defective rolling gates around the perimeter of the Niagara County Jail, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-021-23 on February 21, 2023 to authorize funding of the County portion of the project expense in the 2023 budget, and\nWHEREAS, the upon replacement of the rolling gates, additional issues with encoders were found on the gates which need to be replaced, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has received a quote of $6,968.56 for a change order to the contract, and\nWHEREAS, prior to execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Overhead Door of Greater Erie Change Order No. l may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CW-029-23":"Resolution No. CW-029-23\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nAW ARD OF CONTRACT - OPPENHEIM PARK PAVING AND DRAINAGE\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Oppenheim Park Paving and Drainage Project, and\nWHEREAS, the following bids were publicly opened and read by our Purchasing Department on September 14, 2023 as tabulated below:\n1. Concrete Applied Technologies Corp. (CATCO)  $ 781,798.50\n1266 Townline Road\nAlden, NY 14004\n2. Northeast Diversification, Inc.  $ 829,326.00\n2 Cadby Industrial Park\nLancaster, NY 14086\n3. Milherst Construction  $ 874,713.06\n10025 County Road\nClarence Center, NY 14032\n4. Scott Lawn Yard  $ 889,585.00\n3305 Haseley Drive\nNiagaraFalls,NY 14304\n5. Occhino Corp.  $ 943,435.75\n2650 Seneca Street\nWest Seneca, NY 14224\n6. Edbauer Construction  $ 990,548.53\n2790 Clinton Street\nWest Seneca, NY 14224\n7. American Paving & Excavating  $ 996,984.05\n5880 Thompson Road\nClarence Center, NY 14032\n8. Sutton Paving & Excavating  $1,027,000.00\nP.O. Box 253\nEast Amherst, NY 14051\nand\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nH771.15.7110.000 44089.07 Federal Aid, ARPA Oppenheim Improvements  $500,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH771.15.7110.000 72400.00 Land Improvements Oppenheim Park  $500,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the contract for the Oppenheim Park Paving and Drainage Project be awarded to the lowest responsible bidder, Concrete Applied Technologies Corp. (CATCO), 1266 Townline Road, Alden, NY 14004, in the amount of$781,798.50, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Agreement for the Oppenheim Park Paving and Drainage Project, may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Foti.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CW-031-23":"Resolution No. CW-031-23\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nBUDGET - FUND BALANCE TRANSFER TO\nCOUNTY ATTORNEY'S SPECIAL LITIGATION, CONTRACTUAL EXPENSE LINE\nWHEREAS, as a result of an unanticipated litigation with respect to illegal inunigrants, refugees and/or asylum seekers, funds are needed for this special litigation as the County Attorney's expense line for such legal services have been exhausted and there are outstanding expenses that currently exist, and\nWHEREAS, the unanticipated litigation include the matter of the City of New York v. Niagara County et al, and Palisades Estates et al v. the County of Niagara et al, both matter it was found to be necessary to refer to outside counsel for special litigation to protect the County's best interest, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Attorney's Office administers payment of these bills on behalf of the County,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the following budget transfer be effectuated:\nINCREASE APPROPRJATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.40599.00  Appropriated Fund Balance  $100,000\nINCREASE APPROPRJATIONS:\nA.11.1930.110 74500.01  Special Litigations Contractual  $100,000\nExpense\nMoved by Collins, seconded by Grozio.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","CW-032-23":"Resolution No. CW-032-23\nFrom: Committee of the Whole.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nCLAIM SETTLEMENT MORGAN CHRYSLER, AS PARENT\nAND NATURAL GUARDIAN OF R.K., INFANT AGAINST COUNTY OF NIAGARA ET AL.\nWHEREAS, Niagara County self-insures its general liability coverage, and\nWHEREAS, such loss fund is fully funded and separate from the General fund, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature is authorized under Section 6-N of the General Municipal Law to approve claim settlements in excess of$25, 000.00, and\nWHEREAS, on February 6, 2021 R.K., Infant was injured in an accident at the sledding hill of the Clyde L. Burmaster Park (formerly Bond Lake Park), and\nWHEREAS, R.K., Infant was injured while in the course of sledding and did forcibly strike a tree at the base of the sledding hill, and\nWHEREAS, Morgan Chrysler commenced the law suit against the County of Niagara entitled and captioned as, Morgan Chrysler, Ind. and as mother and natural guardian of R.K., Infant vs. County of Niagara, New York, Town of Lewiston, New York, Index No.: El 76806/2022, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has the opportunity to settle the case entitled and captioned as, Morgan Chrysler, Ind. as mother and natural guardian ofR.K., Infant vs. County of Niagara, New York, Town of Lewiston, New York, Index No.: El 76806/2022, thereby avoiding additional litigation, trial and adverse judicial determinations, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED that the Niagara County Legislature approves the settlement of Morgan Chrysler, Ind. and as mother and natural guardian of R.K., Infant vs. County of Niagara, New York, Town of Lewiston, New York, Index No.: El 76806/2022, for the total amount of $50,000.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the authority to disburse the necessary settlement funds to settle Morgan Chrysler, Ind. as mother and natural guardian of R.K., Infant vs. County of Niagara, New York, Town of Lewiston, New York, Index No.: El 76806/2022, in settlement, is given to the Risk & Insurance Director in the amount of $50,000.00 upon completion and receipt of a General Release for County of Niagara and all other agents and employees of Niagara County and Stipulation of Discontinuance from plaintiffs and Niagara County's counsel.\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","ED-009-23":"Resolution No. ED-009-23\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nAPPROVAL OF LOW COST POWER BENEFIT TO LOCKPORT ICE ARENA AND\nSPORTS CENTER INC. UNDER THE AGREEMENT FOR THE SALE & PURCHASE\nOF NIAGARA PROJECT POWER & ENERGY (ASPNPPE)\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara entered into the Host Community Relicensing and Settlement Agreement (\"HCRSA\") on June 25, 2005, and\nWHEREAS, the HCRSA entitles the County of Niagara to 9 megawatts of low cost power allocation (at 70% load) from the New York Power Authority, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has made the use of this low cost power allocation for economic development purposes as the top priority for that allocation, and\nWHEREAS, there is presently an Agreement for the Sale and Purchase of Niagara Project Power and Energy (\"ASPNPPE\") that has been approved by the County of Niagara and NYP A and the Governor of the State of New York, and\nWHEREAS, the ASPNPPE specifically provides that the County of Niagara could use its low cost power allocation for economic development purposes, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has taken the necessary actions to fully implement the benefits contained in the HCRSA and the ASPNPPE, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara established the Empower Niagara Board by Resolution ED-021-07 to review applications for the use of low cost power for economic development by reviewing applications and to make recommendations to the Legislature of Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has previously awarded to Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. for this same project under resolution ED-011-14, 400 kw (at 70% load factor), and then in 2020 a three-year extension of that agreement was granted under resolution ED-013-20, which will expire August 31, 2023, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of the Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center, Inc. to enter into a third three-year renewal, as the Empower Agreement has created savings to allow them to continue to employee various professional, make capital improvements to the arena/center, and provide economic growth to the community and\nWHEREAS, Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. has met each part of the previous agreement and they have continued to maintain several programs which draws nearly 200,000 visitors through its doors, thus the arena has become a large contributor to the local hotel and restaurant revenue through its programs and community activities, and\nWHEREAS, this approval includes 250 kw previously designated to the Town of Lockport, for its designation with approval of the Empower Niagara Board, and 150 kw solely approved through the Empower Board, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board has reviewed the renewal request from Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. for the use of low cost power for developing, renovating and expanding its business in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. has met all the criteria qualifying its project for consideration and the Empower Niagara Board is recommending approval of an additional three-year extension of the agreement, and\nWHEREAS, the approval of the Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. original application increased economic development in the City of Lockport, Town of Lockport, Pendleton and Niagara County as well as spurring job creation through economic activity within the City of Lockport, and\nWHEREAS, the Empower Niagara Board has recommended the third extension of low cost power allocation application of 400 kw (at 70% load factor) for a period of three (3) years, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby approves the Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. application for a third three-year extension of 400 kw (at 70% load factor) low cost power benefits from Niagara County, pursuant to low cost power allocation received through the June 25, 2005 HCRSA and ASPNPPE, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the Empower User Agreement of the agreement for Low Cost Power with Lockport Ice Arena and Sports Center Inc. for an additional three-year period.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-129-23":"Resolution No. IF-129-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - PARKS MISCELLANEOUS FEES\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works, Parks Division, accepts credit card payments for cart rental, rounds of golf, purchase of golf balls, etc., and\nWHEREAS, the fee for the use of credit cards is assumed by the Parks Department, and\nWHEREAS, due to the increase in golf activities and credit card usage, additional funds are required, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in vacant positions, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPIRATION\nA.15.1440.000 71010.00  Positions - No. 13720  $9,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.15.1375.000 74000.02  Miscellaneous Fees  $9,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-130-23":"Resolution No. IF-130-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nCREATE AND FILL\nONE (1) CLEANER/LABORER TEMPORARY POSITION\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works Buildings and Grounds Division provides janitorial service functions for all county owned office buildings, and\nWHEREAS, DPW needs to provide this service in order to provide a safe and clean environment for workers and visitors to these office areas, and\nWHEREAS, current staff is unable to maintain the additional workload that will be occurring due to a temporary medical leave, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in other vacant positions, to create one(!) six (6) month temporary position to assist staff members during this leave, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that one(!) Cleaner/Laborer Position, AFSCME Job Group 42-561, at a salary of\n$16.35-18.07 be created and filled temporarily for six months, effective September 20, 2023, and be it further\nRESOLVED, the following budget modification be effectuated:\nDECREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.15.1620.000 71010.00  Positions - Buildings Maintenance Mechanic #13351  $7,500\nA.15.1620.000 71010.00  Positions-Building Maintenance Mechanic #13353  $7,500\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.15.1620.000 71010.00  Positions - Cleaner/Laborer #xxxxx  $15,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-131-23":"Resolution No. IF-131-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nABOLISH GENERAL REP AIR PERSON POSITION/CREATE & FILL GENERAL REP AIR\nPERSON II POSITION- BUILDING AND GROUNDS\nWHEREAS, due to personnel vacancies within the Buildings and Grounds Division of the Public Works Department, the department has reviewed its operational needs for maintaining the various office buildings throughout Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, after studying the services we provide and evaluating all options it has been determined that it would be in the best interest of the department, as well as the taxpayers, to abolish one (I) General Repair Person position and to create and fill one (I) General Repair Person II position, and\nWHEREAS, funding for this newly created position will be from the mentioned abolished positions, and a currently vacant Building Maintenance Mechanic position, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Position No. 13595, General Repair Person, be abolished, effective October 16, 2023, and be it further\nRESOLVED that the one (I) position of General Repair Person II, AFSCME, Grade 13, Step I, at an annual salary of$52,805,52, be created and filled, effective October 16, 2023, and be it further\nRESOLVED that the following budget modification be made:\nFROM:\nA.15.1620.000.71010.00  Positions (General Repair Person - Position #13595)  $15,000.00\nA.15.1620.000.71010.00  Positions  $ 1,608.32\n(Building Maintenance Mechanic- Position #13351)\nTO:\nA.15.1620.000.71010.00  Positions (General Repair Person II- Pos. #xxxxx)  $16,608.32\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-132-23":"Resolution No. IF-132-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nCOURT HOUSE HVAC IMPROVEMENTS - PHASE II\nELECTRICAL CONTRACT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-004-23, dated January 17, 2023, awarded the contract for the Court House HV AC Improvements - Phase II Electrical Project to CIR Electrical, 2527 Long Road, Grand Island, NY 14072, for a contract amount not to exceed $160,250, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $80,056.10, for electrical work associated with the addition of electric heat coils in various locations, for a revised contract amount of $240,306.10, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract by $80,056.10 for the Court House HVAC Improvements - Phase II Electrical Project, for a revised contract amount of $240,306.10, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-133-23":"Resolution No. IF-133-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nCOURT HOUSE HVAC IMPROVEMENTS - PHASE II\nMECHANICAL CONTRACT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. CW-005-23, dated January 17, 2023, awarded the contract for the Court House HV AC Improvements - Phase II Mechanical Project to Greater Niagara Mechanical, 7311 Ward Road, North Tonawanda, NY 14120, for a contract amount not to exceed $1,772,500, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $75,651, for mechanical work associated with the addition of electric heat coils in various locations, for a revised contract amount of $1,848,151, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract by $75,651 for the Court House HVAC Improvements- Phase II Mechanical Project, for a revised contract amount of$1,848,151, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-134-23":"Resolution No. IF-134-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nCOURT HOUSE HVAC IMPROVEMENTS - PHASE II\nGENERAL CONSTRUCTION CONTRACT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-051-23, dated March 21, 2023, awarded the contract for the Court House HV AC Improvements - Phase II General Construction Project to D.R. Chamberlain Corp., 50 Simons Street, Lockport, NY 14094, for a contract amount not to exceed $925,735, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in an amount not to exceed $35,157, for general construction work associated with the addition of electric heat coils in various locations, and to repair plaster and paint the 2nd floor court room, for a revised contract amount of $960,892, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 1 to increase the contract in an amount not to exceed $35,157 for the Court House HV AC Improvements - Phase II General Construction Project, for a revised contract amount of $960,892, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-135-23":"Resolution No. IF-135-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nKRULL PARK SHORELINE REHABILITATION PROJECT\nDESIGN SERVICES CONTRACT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 6\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-127-19, dated November 19, 2019, awarded the design services contract for the Krull Park Shoreline Rehabilitation project to WSP USA, 50 Lakefront Blvd, Suite 111, Buffalo, NY 14202, for a contract amount of $375,979, and WHERAS, Resolution No. IF-102-21 dated September 21, 2021, extended the contract completion date to December 31, 2022, at no additional cost to the county, to allow for final design services, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-158-22, dated December 6, 2022, increased the contract by $184,490, for construction administration and construction inspection, for a total contract amount of $560,469, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-004-23, dated January 17, 2023, authorized the extension of the contract to May 31, 2024, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-089-23, dated May 16, 2023, increased the contract by $110,137.50, due to the project schedule being extended due to contractor challenges with the project and the need for additional inspections and site supervision, for a total contract amount of $670,606.50, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-117-23, dated August 1, 2023, increased the contract by $93,458, due to extending the contract to September and contractor challenges adding additional inspections and site supervision, for a total contract amount of $764,064.50, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract by $112,365.88, due to the project schedule being extended to November 3, 2023, due to contractor challenges and the need for additional inspections and site supervision, for a total contract amount of $876,430.38, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to WSP USA, 50 Lakefront Blvd, Suite 111, Buffalo, NY 14202, be, increased by $112,365.88, for a total contract amount of$876,430.38, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the change order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-136-23":"Resolution No. IF-136-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nDRAINAGE FOR NIAGARA COUNTY GOLF COURSE\nDRIVING RANGE PROJECT - CHANGE ORDER NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-127-23, dated August I, 2023, awarded the contract for the Drainage for the Niagara County Golf Course Driving Range Project to Palladino Excavating & Concrete, 5653 Wendy Circle, Lockport, NY 14094, for a contract amount not to exceed $135,000, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to increase the contract in the amount of $41,000 for digging 1,600 feet of a new ditch, installing culverts at the cart crossings, and adding stone around said culverts at the Golf Course, for a revised contract amount of$176,000, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. I to increase the contract by $41,000 for the Drainage for the Niagara County Golf Course Driving Range Project, for a revised contract amount of $176,000, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-137-23":"Resolution No. IF-137-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nSURVEY SERVICES-EWINGS ROAD FROM JACQUES TO ROUTE 78 AND\nEAST CANAL ROAD FROM LOCKPORT TO FEIGLE\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-110-21, dated October 19, 2021, authorized the contract for Survey Services for Ewings Road from Jacques to Route 78 and East Canal Road from Lockport to Feigle Project to Popli Design Group, 555 Penbrooke Drive, Penfield, NY 14526, for a contract amount of$103,594, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to decrease the contract in the amount of $15,616.76, as the project is now complete, for a revised contract amount of $87,977.24, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Amendment No. I-Final to decrease the contract by $15,616.76 for the Survey Services for Ewings Road from Jacques to Route 78 and East Canal Road from Lockport to Feigle Project, for a revised contract amount of $87,977.24, be approved, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-138-23":"Resolution No. IF-138-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nPURPLE HEART VETERANS MONUMENT\nCHANGE ORDER NO. 2-FINAL\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-072-23, dated April 25, 2023, awarded the contract for the Purple Heart Veterans Monument to Mark Cerrone, 2368 Maryland Ave, Niagara Falls, NY 14305, in the amount of $129,000, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-119-23, dated August 1, 2023, authorized Change Order No. 1 to extend the contract date to August 31, 2023, at no additional cost to the County, and\nWHEREAS, the contract needs to be reduced in the amount of $8,000, as the contingency allowance was not used and the project is now complete, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that Change Order No. 2-Final, for the contract for the Purple Heart Veterans Monument project be reduced in the amount of $8,000, for a revised contract amount of $121,000.00, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that final payment is authorized to be made to Mark Cerrone, 2368 Maryland Ave, Niagara Falls, NY 14305, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the required documents may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-139-23":"Resolution No. IF-139-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nAWARD CONSULTANT CONTRACT - UPPER MOUNTAIN ROAD FROM\n700' NORTH OF ROUTE 31 TO LOCKPORT JUNCTION ROAD\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works evaluated proposals from pre-qualified consulting engineering firms to assist the County with design services for the Upper Mountain Road from 700' North of Route 31 to Lockport Junction Road Project, and\nWHEREAS, C & S Engineers provided a proposal for the design of said project, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language, and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE REVENUE:\nH769.15.5112.000 43501.00 Consol Highway Aid Revenue  $30,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nH769.15.5112.000 72600.01 Infrastructure Roads-Upper Mountain Rd.  $30,000\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the design of Upper Mountain Road from 700' North of Route 31 to Lockport Junction Road Project, be awarded to C & S Engineers, 499 Col. Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a contract amount not to exceed $320,000, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-140-23":"Resolution No. IF-140-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION REJECTING BIDS FOR BRIDGE AND HIGHWAY RAIL UPGRADES-\nBRALEY ROAD OVER THE EAST BRANCH OF TWELVE MILE CREEK\nWHEREAS, the Department of Public Works has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for bids for the Bridge and Highway Rail Upgrades at the Braley Road Bridge over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek, and\nWHEREAS, the lowest responsible bidder, Edbauer Construction, 2790 Clinton Street, West Seneca, NY 14224, submitted a bid in the amount of$372,141, and\nWHEREAS, the engineer's estimate for the project was $150,000, and\nWHEREAS, the lowest bid far exceeds the estimate for the project, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the sealed bids which were received on Thursday, July 20, 2023 are hereby rejected and the County of Niagara, Department of Public Works, shall re-advertise for the Bridge and Highway Rail Upgrades at the Braley Road Bridge over the East Branch of Twelve Mile Creek.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-141-23":"Resolution No. IF-141-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nAUTHORIZATION TO COMMENCE ACTION AGAINST\nTHE NEXTERA ENERGY TRANSMISSION NEW YORK\nWHEREAS, NextEra Energy Transmission New York commenced a large construction project on Block Church Road ( the \"Road\") without obtaining a highway permit to do so, at which time the County of Niagara instructed NextEra Energy Transmission New York to cease its operations, and WHREREAS, NextEra Energy Transmission New York requested a permit in response to the County of Niagara's instructions and indicated in writing, that it would repair any damages to the Road after completion of the project, and\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara assessed the Roadway and shoulder area of Block Church Road for repairs and reconstruction and has estimated the repairs to cost $328,137.00, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Attorney's Office and the Department of Public Works have made several attempts to be reimbursed by NextEra Energy Transmission New York, as all costs and expenses incurred as a result of any roadway and shoulder damage, should be paid by NextEra Energy Transmission New York, and\nWHEREAS, NextEra Energy Transmission New York has an obligation to reimburse Niagara County for the repair and reconstruction expenses of Block Church Road damage, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that pursuant to County Law \u00a7501 (3), the Legislature of the County of Niagara hereby directs the Niagara County Attorney to commence legal action against the NextEra Energy Transmission New York seeking the $328,137.00 for the cost and expenses for the repair and reconstruction of Block Church Road which was damage directly resulting from the NextEra Energy Transmission New York operations, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature of the County of Niagara directs the County Attorney to take any and all necessary measures and legal procedures to provide for the legal action commenced by this resolution.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IF-142-23":"Resolution No. IF-142-23\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC WORKS\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - AMERICAN RESCUE PLAN ACT FUNDS\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has received fund through the American Rescue Plan Act Funds to address negative economic impacts caused by the COVID-19 public health emergency, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Office for the Aging utilizes vehicles for a variety of services for the seniors over 60 within Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, funds are required to allow for the replacement of four (4) Office for the Aging vehicles, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modifications be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nDM.15.5132.000 44089.07  Federal Aid, Other ARPA  $176,000\nState/Local Fiscal Recovery Fund\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nDM.15.5132.000 72100.12  Machinery and Equipment Cars, Vans, Light Trucks  $176,000\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-092-23":"Resolution No. IL-092-23\nFrom: Legislator Rebecca J. Wydysh and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE LEWISTON COMMUNITY LIONS CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the mission of the Lewiston Community Lions Club is to empower volunteers to serve their communities, meet humanitarian needs, to encourage peace and promote international understanding, and\nWHEREAS, the Lewiston Community Lions Club volunteers for many different kinds of community projects - including caring for the environment, feeding the hungry, and aiding seniors and the disabled, and\nWHEREAS, their current \"Village of Trees\" project aims to emich public spaces by introducing trees that create natural shaded outdoor areas, promoting the health and well-being of Lewiston Village residents and visitors while enhancing the area's aesthetics, and\nWHEREAS, the project will encompass three publicly accessible properties, including two parks and one cemetery, portions of which serve as venues for various festivals, music events, a farmers market, and other community historical and recreational events, and\nWHEREAS, the \"Village of Trees\" project is an essential endeavor to create inviting and nurturing outdoor spaces for the community, encouraging a sense of unity and appreciation for nature's beauty and historical significance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Lewiston Community Lions Club by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nLewiston Community Lions Club  $2,000.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-093-23":"Resolution No. IL-093-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF NEWFANE PTSA\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Newfane Parent Teacher Student Association (PTSA) goal is to work cooperatively with the Newfane Central School District's Board of Education, Administration, Teachers and Staff to enhance our students' learning experience, and\nWHEREAS, the PTSA participates and hosts many events such as Movie Night, Halloween Trunk or Treat, Holiday Shop, and food concessions at many youth sporting events, and\nWHEREAS, this organization is volunteer and community based, comprising of incredible parents who seek to connect students and parents to the school staff and to enhance activities around the school, and\nWHEREAS, funding is needed in order to continue their efforts as well as expand the services they seek to provide, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Newfane PTSA by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nNewfane PTSA  $1,000.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-094-23":"Resolution No. IL-094-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF SOMERSET CONSERVATION CLUB\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Somerset Conservation Club was founded in 1961 and for over 60 years generations of hunters and outdoor lovers have enjoyed the camaraderie of fellow sportsmen all year long, and\nWHEREAS, the sprawling 35 acre property offers a fishing pond, acres of woods for hunting, a rifle range, trap field, and is available to rent for private parties, and\nWHEREAS, a conservation club is not just about hunting and shooting, the Outdoor Life Conservation Pledge states \"I pledge to protect and conserve the natural resources of America. I promise to educate future generations so they may become caretakers of our water, air, land, and wildlife.\", and\nWHEREAS, conservation clubs are a vital partner in our community and offer our youth lifelong skills and safety training that will benefit them for life, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Somerset Conservation Club by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nSomerset Conservation Club  $500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-095-23":"Resolution No. IL-095-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF BARKER FIRE DEPARTMENT\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Barker Fire Department (BFD) was founded in 1917, and has provided fire and EMS services ever since inception, and\nWHEREAS, BFD members work tirelessly to protect our community and be public servants to our residents during all times of need, and\nWHEREAS, rising costs, Covid pandemic, work and family responsibilities, and increasing regulations from NYS have made it more costly and time consuming to be a volunteer, and\nWHEREAS, additional funds are necessary to offset these rising costs and help support our Niagara County volunteer fire departments, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the volunteer firefighters at Barker Fire Dept by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nBarker Fire Department  $1,500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-096-23":"Resolution No. IL-096-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF MILLER HOSE FIRE COMPANY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Miller Hose Fire Company (MHC) was founded in 1911, and has provided fire and EMS services ever since inception, and\nWHEREAS, MHC members work tirelessly to protect our community and be public servants to our residents during all times of need, and\nWHEREAS, rising costs, Covid pandemic, work and family responsibilities, and increasing regulations from NYS have made it more costly and time consuming to be a volunteer, and\nWHEREAS, additional funds are necessary to offset these rising costs and help support our Niagara County volunteer fire departments, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the volunteer firefighters at Miller Hose Co by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nMiller Hose Fire Company  $1,500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-097-23":"Resolution No. IL-097-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF NEWFANE\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Town of Newfane has a Newfane & Olcott Tourism committee which focuses on showcasing the amazing opportunities to work, eat, play, explore, and have fun within the region, and\nWHEREAS, the Tourism Committee creates an annual Visitors Guide which highlights all of the offerings around the community, and\nWHEREAS, over 20,000 of these guides are printed and distributed around WNY every year, and\nWHEREAS, costs to produce and distribute these guides has increased drastically due to many factors, and\nWHEREAS, funds are necessary to offset cost increases and to continue the promotion of Newfane and Olcott tourism, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Town of Newfane Tourism Board by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nTown of Newfane \"Tourism Committee\"  $1,000.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-098-23":"Resolution No. IL-098-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF OLCOTT FIRE COMPANY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Olcott Fire Company (OFC) was founded in 1928, and has provided fire and EMS services ever since inception, and\nWHEREAS, OFC members work tirelessly to protect our community and be public servants to our residents during all times of need, and\nWHEREAS, rising costs, Covid pandemic, work and family responsibilities, and increasing regulations from NYS have made it more costly and time consuming to be a volunteer, and\nWHEREAS, additional funds are necessary to offset these rising costs and help support our Niagara County volunteer fire departments, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the volunteer firefighters at Olcott Fire Co by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nOlcott Fire Company  $1,500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-099-23":"Resolution No. IL-099-23\nFrom: Legislator Shawn A. Foti and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF WRIGHTS CORNERS FIRE COMP ANY\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Wrights Comers Fire Company (WCFC) was founded in 1939, and has provided fire and EMS services ever since inception, and\nWHEREAS, WCFC members work tirelessly to protect our community and be public servants to our residents during all times of need, and\nWHEREAS, rising costs, Covid pandemic, work and family responsibilities, and increasing regulations from NYS have made it more costly and time consuming to be a volunteer, and\nWHEREAS, additional funds are necessary to offset these rising costs and help support our Niagara County volunteer fire departments, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the volunteer firefighters at Wrights Comers Fire Company by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nWrights Comers Fire Company  $1,500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-100-23":"Resolution No. IL-100-23\nFrom:  Legislators Irene M. Myers, Rebecca J. Wydysh, Richard E. Abbott Shawn A. Foti, Randy R. Brant,\nAnthony J. Nemi, William J. Collins, Mark J. Grozio, Christopher Voccio, Christopher A. Robins and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF OLD FORT NIAGARA\nTHROUGH COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, Old Fort Niagara's history spans more than 300 years and features one of the nation's finest collections of original 18th and early 19th century military architecture and related artifacts depicting people and events throughout the site's history, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County will have the honor to receive wartime medals of US Army Private First-Class Frank Gaffney (12/18-/1883-5/25/1948), and\nWHEREAS, as a Niagara County resident, Frank was regarded as the \"human hurricane\" during his enlistment, with his war time honors include Medal of Honor, Purple Heart and many other honors and awards for his bravery from other countries, and\nWHEREAS, the medals will be kept and displayed in the \"Rotunda\" of the Niagara County Courthouse to honor this war hero, and\nWHEREAS, Old Fort Niagara will make provisions for the keepsake, preservation and display of these medals and other wartime memorabilia, as they continue to educate, share and preserve the history of our Niagara County veterans, wartime heroes, historic structures and memorabilia, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports Old Fort Niagara by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows:\nOld Fort Niagara  $3,000.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-101-23":"Resolution No. IL-101-23\nFrom: Legislator Richard L. Andres.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE PENDLETON ATHLETIC BOOSTER ASSOCIATION\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Pendleton Athletic Boosters Association (PABA), a 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization dedicated to creating and sustaining youth athletic programs of the highest standards in our community, was founded in 1971 to offer programs in youth football, cheerleading, wrestling and, more recently, lacrosse and flag football, and\nWHEREAS, P ABA is an important part of the Pendleton community, offering many children their first opportunity to play organized sports and learn the values of teamwork and sportsmanship; and\nWHEREAS, Community Partnership funds help support numerous organizations across Niagara County that are important to local communities like P ABA, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that funding for the program not allocated in 2022, was assigned to a committed fund balance for future use, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Pendleton Athletic Booster Association by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows:\nPendleton Athletic Booster Association  $500.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to the Niagara County Economic Development 2023 budget: INCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE:\nA.28.8020.814 40599.01  Appropriated Fund Balance - Committed Funds  $500.00\nINCREASE APPROPRIATION:\nA.28.8020.814 74500.01  Community Partnership Fund Expense  $500.00\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 11 Ayes, 0 Noes, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-102-23":"Resolution No. IL-102-23\nFrom: Legislator Richard L. Andres.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nA RESOLUTION CALLING ON GOVERNOR HOCHUL TO VETO A.04282B/S.3505B\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature believes that increasing participation in the election process and reducing costs of government operations is good for our republic, and\nWHEREAS, during the 2023 legislative session, Assembly Member Amy Paulin and State Senator James Skoufis reintroduced this legislation, which would move local elections to even-numbered years, and\nWHEREAS, versions of this legislation were introduced in previous years and failed to reach the floor for a vote due to lack of support, and\nWHEREAS, after the 2022 session concluded, the bill sponsor indicated that hearings would be held on the proposal through the Election Committee process, and\nWHEREAS, no such hearings were held, and\nWHEREAS, the proposed legislation circumvented the legislative committee process during the legislative session, limiting public input and understanding of the impacts, and\nWHEREAS, there has been no financial analysis or operational analysis of the impacts on local Boards of Elections, and\nWHEREAS, this legislation passed in the middle of the night on the last days of the legislative session, which is counter to the idea of an open and transparent government, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature has many concerns with this bill, especially its dirninishment of adequate attention to local issues and local voices, and\nWHEREAS, by forcing local elections to compete against national and state-level campaigns, this legislation will decrease voters' attention to important local issues and deprive local candidates of appropriate platforms to explain their stance on issues, and\nWHEREAS, moving local elections to even-numbered years would increase the cost of campaigns for local office due to advertising prices being driven up by demand from national and state level campaigns, thereby reducing the ability of local campaigns to get their messages out, and\nWHEREAS, this legislation excludes elections to offices in New York City and all other cities and villages in New York State, as well as certain offices within all counties and towns, thereby negating any possible savings to local Boards of Election, and\nWHEREAS, this legislation usurps Home Rule powers reserved by local governments and protected by the Constitution of the State ofNew York, raising questions oflegality and constitutional authority, and\nWHEREAS, local municipalities have had the ability to change their elections to even-numbered years for decades, and have chosen not to do so, and\nWHEREAS, establishing the schedule of local elections is the right of the local government, supported by more than a century of precedent, and\nWHEREAS, the Assembly Bill sponsor indicated that she had not received any messages of support for this proposal from any of the 57 Counties and 933 Towns across New York State impacted by this legislation, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature calls on Governor Hochul to strongly oppose this legislation and utilize her veto authority to reject this proposal, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Niagara County Legislature be, and hereby is, directed to forward cettified copies of this resolution to Governor Kathy Hochul, Senate Majority Leader Andrea Stewart Cousins, Assembly Speaker Carl Heastie, Senate Minority Leader Robert Ortt, Assembly Minority Leader William Barclay, Assembly Bill Sponsor Amy Paulin, Senate Bill Sponsor James Skoufis, Assemblyman Angelo Morinello, Assemblyman Michael Norris, as well as to any others deemed necessary and appropriate.\nMoved by Andres, seconded by Collins, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 8 Ayes, 3 Noes - Elder, Grozio, Robins, 4 Absent- Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers.","IL-103-23":"Resolutiou No. IL-103-23\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey.\nDated: September 19, 2023\nA RESOLUTION TO SHARE THE SERVICES OF PUBLIC INFORMATION OFFICER WITH\nORLEANS COUNTY\nWHEREAS, communicating county information is an important function of county government as recognized when Niagara County created the office of Public Information Office, and\nWHEREAS, Orleans County has a need for the same function of Public Information Officer, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County and Orleans County have a long history of collaboration, as exemplified by our Niagara Orleans Regional Alliance and our Niagara Orleans Regional Land Improvement Corporation, and\nWHEREAS, Orleans County desires to share the services of the Niagara County Public Information Officer, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, Niagara County is authorized to enter into an agreement with Orleans County for PIO services, and\nbe it further\nRESOLVED, the PIO position will be increased from 35 hours per week to 40 hour per week to provide the services of a Public Information Officer to Orleans County effective September 27, 2023 through September 26, 2025, at a total cost not to exceed $19,600.00 for year one with a COLA for year two, for the commitment of 5 hours per week to Orleans County.\nApproved for Submission.\nMoved by N emi, seconded by Abbott, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Nemi, seconded by Abbott.\nAdopted. 8 Ayes, 3 Noes - Elder, Grozio, Robins, 4 Absent - Bradt, Godfrey, Hill, Myers."};
